00:02:20.580 --> 00:02:25.980
and Coca Cola or someone like Johnson and&nbsp;
Johnson. But, these aren’t tech companies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.980 --> 00:02:31.260
so they don’t exactly have a bunch of data&nbsp;
on you like Oracle. A couple of years ago,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.260 --> 00:02:36.420
Larry Ellison even made a presentation in which&nbsp;
he made his intentions of controlling the entire&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.420 --> 00:02:43.020
population blatantly obvious. He said quote&nbsp;
“How many people are on Earth? Seven billion,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.020 --> 00:02:50.760
Two billion to go.” On my last video about SAP, a&nbsp;
lot people commented that SAP isn’t actually all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.760 --> 00:02:56.220
that powerful given that they simply sell software&nbsp;
to companies, but like I said in that video,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.220 --> 00:03:03.120
that’s because SAP doesn’t do anything nefarious&nbsp;
with it even though they could. And Oracle is a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.120 --> 00:03:08.640
perfect example of just what they could do. Just&nbsp;
a few months ago, a class action lawsuit was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.640 --> 00:03:16.560
brought against Oracle for allegedly collecting,&nbsp;
profiling, and selling the data of 5B users. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.560 --> 00:03:24.420
here’s the story of how Oracle became the most&nbsp;
discrete giant within the entire tech industry.

00:03:24.420 --> 00:03:28.380
THE ORIGIN OF ORACLE:&nbsp;

00:03:28.380 --> 00:03:35.460
Taking a look back, the story of Oracle dates&nbsp;
back to 1977 to three men named Bob Miner,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.460 --> 00:03:41.520
Ed Oates, and Larry Ellison. The company started&nbsp;
off as a database software developer. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.520 --> 00:03:48.300
the company’s original name was actually Software&nbsp;
Development Laboratories or SDL. The inspiration&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.300 --> 00:03:53.640
for the company was from a research paper&nbsp;
written by Edgar F Codd that described the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.640 --> 00:03:58.800
intricacies of relational database management&nbsp;
systems. At first, Ellison’s goal was to create&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.800 --> 00:04:04.560
a piece of software that was compatible for&nbsp;
IBM’s System R Database. But unlike Xerox,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.560 --> 00:04:10.560
IBM wasn’t absolute idiots who just handed out&nbsp;
their intellectual property like candy. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.560 --> 00:04:15.900
the trio would have an extremely difficult time&nbsp;
trying to troubleshoot error codes and they would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.900 --> 00:04:21.060
eventually shift their focus onto an easier&nbsp;
target: the government. The US government has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.060 --> 00:04:26.220
this policy of giving everyone an equal chance&nbsp;
of trying to land government contracts. They&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.220 --> 00:04:31.800
post their contracts publicly and virtually any&nbsp;
US citizen can bid on these contracts. The trio&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.800 --> 00:04:38.160
would do exactly this and somehow they would win&nbsp;
a contract to build a database from the CIA. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.160 --> 00:04:43.560
project was called Oracle and this is what would&nbsp;
eventually become the company’s name. With a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.560 --> 00:04:47.940
project under their belt, the team would get to&nbsp;
work on creating the world’s first commercial&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.940 --> 00:04:53.940
relational database program that used SQL. And&nbsp;
this program would eventually get deployed at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.940 --> 00:04:59.400
an airforce base in Dayton, Ohio. Fun fact, the&nbsp;
first commercial version of their product was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.400 --> 00:05:04.980
actually called Oracle V2. The team figured that&nbsp;
less people would be willing to buy a V1 product,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.980 --> 00:05:10.980
so they just went ahead and named it V2. And it&nbsp;
seems like this along with the fact that they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.980 --> 00:05:16.320
serviced the CIA gave them quite a solid&nbsp;
reputation. Not to mention, it’s not like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.320 --> 00:05:21.720
there were many other options anyway, so more and&nbsp;
more companies started to adopt Oracle for their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.720 --> 00:05:27.540
database software. This was really the bread and&nbsp;
butter of the original Oracle. Year after year,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.540 --> 00:05:33.720
they just kept iterating on their database&nbsp;
software coming out with Oracle V3, V4, V5,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.720 --> 00:05:39.480
V6, V7, and so on. This was quite lucrative&nbsp;
given that tech companies were rapidly growing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.480 --> 00:05:44.040
and basically all of them needed database&nbsp;
software which they didn’t want to develop&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.040 --> 00:05:49.620
themselves. By 1989, they reached annual&nbsp;
revenues of over half a billion dollars,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.620 --> 00:05:55.320
but Oracle was just getting started. Throughout&nbsp;
the 1990s, they would try to expand their business&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.320 --> 00:06:02.340
and move away from databases, but this didn’t&nbsp;
work out all that well. For example, in 1995,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.340 --> 00:06:06.600
they tried to break into the internet market&nbsp;
by launching a browser called PowerBrowser.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.600 --> 00:06:11.400
Similarly, in 1997, they tried to break into&nbsp;
the computer market by launching something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.400 --> 00:06:17.880
called the network computer. The network computer&nbsp;
was actually a brilliant idea. It aimed to remove&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.880 --> 00:06:22.620
much of the components inside of a computer&nbsp;
and instead have it run off servers, networks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.620 --> 00:06:27.720
and databases similar to a chromebook. But, Oracle&nbsp;
quickly found out that the public just wasn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.720 --> 00:06:32.580
ready for this product. While these products&nbsp;
didn’t quite work out, Oracle’s core business&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.580 --> 00:06:38.220
was still growing exponentially, and soon they&nbsp;
actually found a viable expansion opportunity.

00:06:38.220 --> 00:06:42.360
ORACLE PRODUCTS:&nbsp;

00:06:42.360 --> 00:06:47.580
While Oracle wasn’t able to break into those new&nbsp;
markets, something they could do is buy companies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.580 --> 00:06:52.800
that were already successful. And luckily for&nbsp;
Oracle, they would get the buying opportunity of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.800 --> 00:06:58.140
a lifetime with the Dotcom crash. While Oracle’s&nbsp;
stock also got destroyed by the dotcom crash,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.140 --> 00:07:03.420
the same could not be said about Oracle’s&nbsp;
fundamentals. Their business itself was as strong&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.420 --> 00:07:08.460
as ever which gave them the perfect opportunity&nbsp;
to buyout everyone else at record low prices,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.460 --> 00:07:14.880
and that’s exactly what they did. Oracle’s entire&nbsp;
acquisition history is way too long to actually go&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.880 --> 00:07:20.520
through, so we’ll just focus on their five&nbsp;
biggest acquisitions starting with PeopleSoft in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.520 --> 00:07:26.880
2005. PeopleSoft was an ERP or enterprise resource&nbsp;
planning company that focused on creating software&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.880 --> 00:07:32.640
for common business tasks. Their product line&nbsp;
included 5 main pieces of software including a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.640 --> 00:07:37.020
human resource management system, a financial&nbsp;
management solution, supply chain management,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.020 --> 00:07:41.700
customer relationship management, and&nbsp;
enterprise performance management. These&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.700 --> 00:07:46.080
products accomplish exactly what they sound&nbsp;
like. The supply chain management software&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.080 --> 00:07:51.840
for example helps companies keep track of demand,&nbsp;
supply, inventory, procurement, order management,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:51.840 --> 00:07:57.540
and so on. Similarly, the human resources system&nbsp;
helps companies keep track of new hires, employee&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:57.540 --> 00:08:04.080
turnover, vacation hours, payroll, benefits, and&nbsp;
so on. I think you can see why basically every&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.080 --> 00:08:09.120
company would need this software. Oracle would&nbsp;
follow up this acquisition by purchasing Siebel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.120 --> 00:08:15.240
Systems. Siebel was also an enterprise software&nbsp;
company, but they didn’t sell ERP software.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:15.240 --> 00:08:21.120
Instead they sold a different branch of enterprise&nbsp;
software called CRM applications or customer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.120 --> 00:08:25.920
relationship management applications. This&nbsp;
software helps companies manage and coordinate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.920 --> 00:08:30.540
their marketing and customer service efforts.&nbsp;
Moving onto Oracle’s 3rd major acquisition,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:30.540 --> 00:08:36.900
we have yet another enterprise solutions company&nbsp;
called BEA systems. BEA systems focused on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.900 --> 00:08:43.020
offering a suite of business process management&nbsp;
tools with an emphasis on e commerce. The even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:43.020 --> 00:08:47.880
called themselves the E-Commerce Transactions&nbsp;
Company as they specialized in creating software&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.880 --> 00:08:53.580
and servers tha allowed for high volume e-commerce&nbsp;
transactions. After that, we have Oracle’s most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:53.580 --> 00:09:00.240
famous acquisition of all time: Sun Microsystems.&nbsp;
Unlike the other companies, Sun wasn’t just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:00.240 --> 00:09:06.480
focused on one product. They were a massive tech&nbsp;
conglomerate that did everything from developing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:06.480 --> 00:09:11.700
software to selling hardware. Likely the most&nbsp;
interesting part of Sun, however, is their various&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:11.700 --> 00:09:16.920
inventions. These were the guys who created the&nbsp;
Java programming language, the Solaris operating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.920 --> 00:09:24.540
system, ZFS, NFS, and SPARC microprocessors. They&nbsp;
also made significant contributions to Unix, RISC&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:24.540 --> 00:09:30.300
processors, thin client computing, and virtualized&nbsp;
computing. Essentially, this acquisition gave&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.300 --> 00:09:37.920
Oracle access to a boatload of IP. And that brings&nbsp;
us into Oracle’s last major acquisition which was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:37.920 --> 00:09:44.280
Netsuite in 2016. Netsuite rounded out Oracle’s&nbsp;
enterprise suite by giving them access to the last&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.280 --> 00:09:49.740
piece that they were missing: the cloud. Netsuite&nbsp;
was an expert at cloud based enterprise software,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:49.740 --> 00:09:55.133
cloud computing, and cloud ERP. And all of these&nbsp;
acquisitions bring us to where we are today.

00:09:55.133 --> 00:09:55.800
ORACLE TODAY:&nbsp;

00:09:59.940 --> 00:10:06.180
If you’re still wondering what Oracle sells, the&nbsp;
answer is virtually anything and everything that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.180 --> 00:10:11.040
companies need to stay operational on the backend.&nbsp;
They started off with just database software,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:11.040 --> 00:10:15.900
but they have gone on to acquire basically every&nbsp;
enterprise solutions giant in the world except&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:15.900 --> 00:10:23.640
for SAP. And today, they offer all of this. As&nbsp;
this Quora user put it, “it would take a very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:23.640 --> 00:10:28.980
dedicated effort for a large organization like a&nbsp;
Fortune 500 to not be running Oracle somewhere.”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:28.980 --> 00:10:35.880
And that’s how Oracle gained access to data of&nbsp;
5 billion people. But what exactly are they even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:35.880 --> 00:10:42.300
collecting? Well, allegedly, Oracle has been using&nbsp;
cookies, tracking pixels, device identification,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:42.300 --> 00:10:47.580
and cross device tracking to collect three subsets&nbsp;
of information starting with personal information.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:47.580 --> 00:10:53.520
This includes users’ names, home and work address,&nbsp;
email address, and phone numbers. The second&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:53.520 --> 00:10:58.440
subset of information consists of behavioral&nbsp;
information. This includes websites viewed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:58.440 --> 00:11:04.680
by users, online and offline purchase, where they&nbsp;
shop, and their mode of payment. The third subset&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:04.680 --> 00:11:09.960
of information is even more personal given that it&nbsp;
includes things such as income, political views,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:09.960 --> 00:11:16.500
and hobbies. And the worst part is that you&nbsp;
virtually have no way of avoiding them. Heck,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:16.500 --> 00:11:22.440
people usually complain about large corporations,&nbsp;
but even they can’t avoid using Oracle. Oracle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:22.440 --> 00:11:27.780
has become an omnipresent backend giant&nbsp;
that’s reputable, has scale, is reliable,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:27.780 --> 00:11:34.740
and has basically everything you would need. So,&nbsp;
Oracle isn’t going anywhere anytime soon. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:34.740 --> 00:11:41.820
they’re only growing stronger and more powerful.&nbsp;
As Ellison said, only 2 billion people to go. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:41.820 --> 00:11:47.760
what about the class action lawsuit? That should&nbsp;
be able to deal some damage to Oracle right? Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:47.760 --> 00:11:53.820
probably not. Even if Oracle ends up being found&nbsp;
guilty, it’s not like a fine would really change&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:53.820 --> 00:12:00.240
their ways or stop them. Even if Oracle was&nbsp;
fined $10 billion, it would at max set them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:00.240 --> 00:12:06.300
back one year. So, there’s really no choice but&nbsp;
to simply deal with it just like we deal with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:06.300 --> 00:12:12.300
Google or Facebook. On the brightside, Oracle&nbsp;
does make our modern tech based world possible,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:12.300 --> 00:12:18.420
but with that being said, it’s probably still&nbsp;
not all that great for an individual company&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:18.420 --> 00:12:25.980
to have so much power, control, and influence, but&nbsp;
that’s just what I think. Did you realize how much&nbsp;&nbsp;

